    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** (78.80) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) anchor our offensive strategy as our highest-scoring assets. Both benefit from a massive +14% park factor and favorable matchups against Freeland and Kelly, respectively. **Mickey Moniak** is particularly compelling today; beyond the park boost, he holds a significant platoon advantage and faces a slow delivery from Kelly that optimizes his potential for a stolen base, which remains our primary focus in this 5x5 format. Similarly, **James Wood** (68.08) is a must-start at leadoff, buoyed by a strong 0.380 xwOBA and a +10% order bonus, giving him the best runway to contribute across all five categories.\n\nWe are leaning heavily into the platoon advantage for **Josh Naylor** (73.26) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (69.35), both of whom take advantage of suspect xERAs from V\u00e1squez and May. **Brice Turang** (56.96) remains a high-floor play despite a tough matchup against Ryan, as a favorable wind and his leadoff-adjacent spot in the order justify his inclusion. The tightest decision on the bench was **Austin Riley** (44.09), who fell just outside the starting threshold. While his talent is undeniable, the steep penalty from facing a sub-3.00 xERA arm and a disadvantageous platoon kept him benched in favor of higher-efficiency matchups like **Gabriel Moreno** and **Dalton Rushing**. \n\nRisk management remains a priority for the backend of the lineup. **Zach McKinstry** (44.55) and **Cole Young** (46.64) are currently hovering at the bottom of our playable range. We are closely monitoring weather and lineup confirmations for these two, as any negative shift in conditions or a drop in the batting order could see them pulled to preserve our game caps. With **Jose Caballero** and **Giancarlo Stanton** sidelined, the depth of our bench\u2014including **Travis Bazzana**\u2014is being tested, so we will remain conservative with our remaining starters to ensure we don't bleed efficiency on low-impact days.",
